00:12:57,264 --> 00:13:04,050
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, at any point, that could be undermined by something that you didn't know.

104
00:13:04,911 --> 00:13:16,257
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if virtue depended on, like, making the right choice in that external sense, at any point, like, it could be proven... what you did could be proven wrong.

105
00:13:16,918 --> 00:13:24,862
[SPEAKER_02]: But if it always depends only on your state of mind, then the whole world can come and tell you you made a mistake, and you know that that's not the case.

106
00:13:25,342 --> 00:13:32,446
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, it's a mistake in a worldly sense, sure, but in the sense of acting out of craving, you would be able to 100%

107
00:13:34,375 --> 00:13:37,697
[SPEAKER_02]: With 100% certainty, say, no, I did not act out of craving.

108
00:13:37,797 --> 00:13:42,118
[SPEAKER_02]: I did not commit an offense in the context of dhamma.

109
00:13:42,979 --> 00:13:50,262
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what the kind of accomplishment and virtue that a person should develop.

110
00:13:50,302 --> 00:13:53,023
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's really what extreme entry is.

111
00:13:53,163 --> 00:13:57,125
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the ability to have that perfect certainty of wholesome and unwholesome.

112
00:13:57,705 --> 00:13:59,266
[SPEAKER_02]: And most importantly, regard to virtue.

113
00:14:00,126 --> 00:14:03,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Because your virtue doesn't depend anymore on the

