340
00:34:25,094 --> 00:34:25,214
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

341
00:34:25,255 --> 00:34:27,736
[SPEAKER_03]: It's always, it's always pushing you towards something.

342
00:34:27,956 --> 00:34:28,156
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

343
00:34:28,377 --> 00:34:29,958
[SPEAKER_03]: And then you can always avoid that.

344
00:34:32,209 --> 00:34:42,540
[SPEAKER_01]: And so perhaps it's worthwhile repeating that when the Buddha discusses wholesome and unwholesome, he always first says avoid the unwholesome and then develop the wholesome.

345
00:34:42,580 --> 00:34:42,760
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

346
00:34:43,001 --> 00:34:44,002
[SPEAKER_03]: It's never the other way around.

347
00:34:44,022 --> 00:34:44,162
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

348
00:34:44,242 --> 00:34:48,306
[SPEAKER_03]: Even the four right efforts, the order that it comes, it says here among

349
00:34:50,709 --> 00:34:56,754
[SPEAKER_03]: So, actually, there's one sutta that he gives like a label to each of his four steps, quote-unquote.

350
00:34:57,154 --> 00:35:01,418
[SPEAKER_03]: The first one is, he prevents unarisen and wholesome states from arising.

351
00:35:01,538 --> 00:35:01,758
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

352
00:35:01,818 --> 00:35:02,759
[SPEAKER_03]: That's called restraint.

353
00:35:03,520 --> 00:35:06,122
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, he abandons the arisen and wholesome states.

354
00:35:06,702 --> 00:35:07,763
[SPEAKER_03]: That's called dispelling.

355
00:35:08,524 --> 00:35:12,405
[SPEAKER_03]: He gives rise to the unarisen unwholesome states.

356
00:35:12,445 --> 00:35:13,005
[SPEAKER_03]: Wholesome states?

357
00:35:13,625 --> 00:35:14,666
[SPEAKER_03]: Unarisen wholesome states, yes.

358
00:35:16,266 --> 00:35:18,367
[SPEAKER_03]: That's called development.

359
00:35:18,847 --> 00:35:23,328
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, he protects the wholesome states that are already there.

360
00:35:23,528 --> 00:35:26,809
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's called, like, protection.

361
00:35:27,149 --> 00:35:31,050
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you... it has to be, obviously,

362
00:35:32,770 --> 00:35:33,911
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like a structural thing.

363
00:35:34,111 --> 00:35:45,522
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't... if there is unwholesome there, and you try to cultivate wholesome directly, it's going to be rooted in the same craving to get rid of the thing that was bothering you.

364
00:35:45,922 --> 00:35:50,987
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the wholesomeness can only come when the unwholesomeness is not there anymore.

365
00:35:51,748 --> 00:35:53,830
[SPEAKER_03]: Otherwise, like we said, that's your being.

366
00:35:54,930 --> 00:35:57,032
[SPEAKER_03]: The unwholesomeness is your being.

367
00:35:57,092 --> 00:36:03,777
[SPEAKER_03]: So, any choice that you make is going to be within that unwholesomeness.

368
00:36:03,797 --> 00:36:14,224
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even the choice to fabricate loving-kindness and give all your possessions up or contemplate dhamma, that's going to be done with the unwholesomeness.

369
00:36:14,645 --> 00:36:17,326
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't get rid of something by

370
00:36:18,387 --> 00:36:24,850
[SPEAKER_03]: Covering it up in that manner, you have to... because the problem is always the resistance to the present feeling.

371
00:36:25,851 --> 00:36:29,733
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, if you're doing these things to get rid of the present feeling,

372
00:36:30,570 --> 00:36:32,211
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you think that that's what the dhamma is.

373
00:36:32,231 --> 00:36:33,773
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, I must get rid of this pressure.

374
00:36:34,853 --> 00:36:35,554
[SPEAKER_03]: That's unwholesome.

375
00:36:35,694 --> 00:36:41,379
[SPEAKER_03]: Even though, externally, quote-unquote, the action looks very wholesome.

376
00:36:41,419 --> 00:36:47,864
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, look, it's the loving-kindness, and it's the renunciation, and it's the contemplation and wisdom that the Buddha talked about.

377
00:36:47,944 --> 00:36:52,347
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's still underlain by the same desire to get rid of pain.

378
00:36:53,168 --> 00:36:55,930
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what makes it wrong.

379
00:36:56,991 --> 00:37:11,475
[SPEAKER_04]: So, like, if someone finds himself, like, with this kind of pressure of wanting to do something, if, like, he cannot just say no but look for something else to do, like, like he feels that kind of a pressure to, I don't know, like...

380
00:37:12,245 --> 00:37:13,745
[SPEAKER_04]: Whatever, like, he wants to do.

381
00:37:13,885 --> 00:37:21,407
[SPEAKER_04]: If you cannot just say no, but want to do something, it will be still rooted in this kind of pressure, whatever it tries to be.

382
00:37:21,767 --> 00:37:22,288
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

383
00:37:22,408 --> 00:37:23,288
[SPEAKER_03]: You can say that way.

384
00:37:23,308 --> 00:37:31,170
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if the mind... if you can't say no to it, then you can see this is an unwholesome pressure.

385
00:37:32,505 --> 00:37:36,507
[SPEAKER_03]: It's something that, if you say no, your mind starts complaining.

386
00:37:37,608 --> 00:37:38,729
[SPEAKER_03]: That's an unwholesome pressure.

387
00:37:39,189 --> 00:37:40,710
[SPEAKER_03]: So...

388
00:37:41,350 --> 00:37:43,371
[SPEAKER_03]: But that, in and of itself, that doesn't mean like...

389
00:37:45,903 --> 00:37:47,725
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to do something about it directly.

390
00:37:47,965 --> 00:37:50,468
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to make sure that you don't act out of it.

391
00:37:50,508 --> 00:37:52,089
[SPEAKER_02]: Like I said, that's how you don't feed it.

392
00:37:52,950 --> 00:38:03,240
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, the mind might be wanting to do something, but as long as you don't do that, that the mind wants, or anything else that... Like, you would know.

393
00:38:03,380 --> 00:38:05,282
[SPEAKER_03]: That's only something that you can know internally.

394
00:38:05,762 --> 00:38:07,504
[SPEAKER_03]: Which actions would be

395
00:38:08,749 --> 00:38:13,231
[SPEAKER_03]: Going further with this pressure and which actions are kind of neutral.

396
00:38:13,711 --> 00:38:17,293
[SPEAKER_03]: It would totally depend on the present situation.

397
00:38:17,373 --> 00:38:18,894
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not something that you could fix down.

398
00:38:19,334 --> 00:38:32,000
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, like I said, you might have a sensual thought and, okay, going to the park on a certain occasion, for most people, most of the time, you could say that would be an action that won't be proliferating the sensual state further.

399
00:38:32,040 --> 00:38:32,180
[SPEAKER_03]: But

400
00:38:32,960 --> 00:38:38,381
[SPEAKER_03]: Maybe, deep down, you know that when you go to the park, there's going to be, you know, attractive people there, or whatever.

401
00:38:38,881 --> 00:38:54,324
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can't even fix that, because there might be a case where... Now, let's say, this first time you went to the park, it wasn't rooted in sensuality, but then, when you went there, you actually encountered, you know, sensually pleasing things.

402
00:38:54,484 --> 00:38:57,385
[SPEAKER_03]: So, now, the next time, your mind is

403
00:38:58,545 --> 00:39:02,989
[SPEAKER_03]: Going to the park is going to be one of the things that your mind wants to do, on account of sensuality.

404
00:39:03,069 --> 00:39:05,671
[SPEAKER_03]: So now, you can't give in to that.

405
00:39:05,731 --> 00:39:11,496
[SPEAKER_03]: It's always dependent on what is in the present situation.

406
00:39:11,616 --> 00:39:19,022
[SPEAKER_03]: Only you, for yourself, would be able to know, as I already repeated, whether you act it out or not.

407
00:39:19,043 --> 00:39:21,825
[SPEAKER_03]: It always basically requires you to be

408
00:39:22,820 --> 00:39:34,391
[SPEAKER_03]: on guard, because it might sound as if on one level, you know, I'm talking about how people would be too wound up with trying to avoid everything.

409
00:39:34,451 --> 00:39:40,317
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, it might sound like this is a more relaxed approach, which, yes, you kind of relax yourself

410
00:39:41,358 --> 00:39:43,080
[SPEAKER_03]: From doing the stuff that doesn't matter.

411
00:39:43,961 --> 00:39:47,644
[SPEAKER_03]: But, on the level that matters, it's actually more strict.

412
00:39:48,325 --> 00:39:56,653
[SPEAKER_03]: Because then, you are forced to... like, if there is that unwholesome state, there's absolutely no justification.

413
00:40:00,669 --> 00:40:07,898
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no extenuating circumstance where you can give in to it and say, oh, but that wasn't unwholesome.

414
00:40:08,138 --> 00:40:08,298
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

415
00:40:08,518 --> 00:40:12,042
[SPEAKER_03]: If you give in, you are acting out of unwholesome.

416
00:40:12,943 --> 00:40:16,688
[SPEAKER_03]: But with a rule, when you take it on the level of external rules and stuff,

417
00:40:17,088 --> 00:40:18,249
[SPEAKER_03]: There can always be exceptions.

418
00:40:18,469 --> 00:40:25,133
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, even in the in the vinaya, like, in some situation, if it's like this, or if it's like that, well, then it's not an offense.

419
00:40:25,874 --> 00:40:26,834
[SPEAKER_03]: But with this, it's not.

420
00:40:26,874 --> 00:40:30,257
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if there is the unwholesome state, and you act it out of it, that's it.

421
00:40:30,737 --> 00:40:32,158
[SPEAKER_03]: Nothing else needs to be considered.

422
00:40:32,198 --> 00:40:32,378
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

423
00:40:32,598 --> 00:40:33,879
[SPEAKER_03]: That was an unwholesome intention.

424
00:40:33,999 --> 00:40:36,941
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the more fundamental criteria.

425
00:40:36,981 --> 00:40:37,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

426
00:40:37,641 --> 00:40:43,225
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's even what Sutta, I remember, where some monk had difficulty remembering all the rules, and he went to the Buddha,

427
00:40:43,875 --> 00:40:47,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha said, well, do you see wholesome and unwholesome?

428
00:40:48,137 --> 00:40:48,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

429
00:40:48,638 --> 00:40:51,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Then Buddha said, just don't act out of unwholesome by body, speech and mind.

430
00:40:51,739 --> 00:40:52,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you do that?

431
00:40:52,720 --> 00:40:53,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, the monk says.

432
00:40:53,980 --> 00:40:56,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the sutra ends by saying that monk didn't do anything unwholesome.

433
00:40:56,882 --> 00:40:58,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

434
00:40:58,363 --> 00:41:04,266
[SPEAKER_02]: Initially, there was no rules who will basically follow that principle in general.

435
00:41:06,625 --> 00:41:14,290
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and it's... you start off by having these precepts and, you know, on some... you have to start somewhere.

436
00:41:14,330 --> 00:41:17,692
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you will be start... starting with the avoidance of

437
00:41:18,870 --> 00:41:36,455
[SPEAKER_03]: Unwholesome states, sorry, of external behaviors, because, practically speaking, if you haven't been keeping the precepts, there will most certainly also be unwholesome pressures making you want to break them, like when you're starting out.

438
00:41:36,915 --> 00:41:44,997
[SPEAKER_03]: So, kind of by accident almost, like without really seeing the level where unwholesome, wholesome and unwholesome is,

439
00:41:45,762 --> 00:41:52,443
[SPEAKER_03]: You would still kind of be withdrawing yourself from it, just by keeping the precepts initially.

440
00:41:53,063 --> 00:41:58,945
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, there will come a point where you... like, keeping the precepts is not painful anymore.

441
00:41:59,525 --> 00:42:01,685
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not really taking you... taking effort.

442
00:42:02,385 --> 00:42:11,427
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, you need to start looking further and see that now... now, keeping the precepts in and of itself,

443
00:42:12,800 --> 00:42:14,980
[SPEAKER_03]: Because there isn't that pressure anymore to break them.

444
00:42:15,321 --> 00:42:21,122
[SPEAKER_03]: Meaning, the pressure needs to have moved somewhere else now, unless you're an arahant.

445
00:42:22,022 --> 00:42:33,824
[SPEAKER_02]: There must be something else now that your mind is pushing you to do, and you might be justifying it as this is fine, because it's... Look, I'm not breaking any rules.

446
00:42:33,964 --> 00:42:36,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so then what would be, like, can we say that

447
00:42:37,388 --> 00:42:40,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

448
00:42:40,852 --> 00:42:52,123
[SPEAKER_03]: So, at that point, like, you might... And that's actually a point that many monks and people in general, who take on the practice of renunciation, might not go beyond.

449
00:42:52,544 --> 00:42:52,724
[SPEAKER_03]: Like,

450
00:42:54,116 --> 00:43:01,799
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, you might think, oh, because I'm already celibate, I'm already restrained, so there's a...

451
00:43:02,799 --> 00:43:09,382
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know, the monks are having a meeting at 5 p.m. to drink tea and just chat and socialize.

452
00:43:09,982 --> 00:43:20,966
[SPEAKER_03]: And you would think, well, all of us are, you know, celibate living in this community, we don't talk about unwholesome things, we don't talk about sensuality, but, you know,

453
00:43:21,606 --> 00:43:27,830
[SPEAKER_01]: But you could go there out of the desire to delight in company, for example, which is a more subtle form of unwholesome.

454
00:43:27,890 --> 00:43:28,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

455
00:43:28,331 --> 00:43:35,315
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then it would be unwholesome, even if everyone is, you know, perfectly well-behaved and stuff.

456
00:43:37,147 --> 00:43:46,673
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there is that pressure, it would be obviously a more subtle form, but it's still the same thing.

457
00:43:46,713 --> 00:44:01,683
[SPEAKER_03]: As the Buddha said, it's even like a little... if you take a... how do you say... with your fingernail, you scoop a little bit of excrement, it smells just as bad as a full bucket.

458
00:44:02,745 --> 00:44:10,160
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even these very subtle things, you can't justify them, because it's...

459
00:44:12,763 --> 00:44:17,847
[SPEAKER_03]: Just by doing that, that would already prevent you from the basic sotāpati.

460
00:44:17,867 --> 00:44:22,411
[SPEAKER_03]: Sotāpati is knowing wholesome is wholesome and unwholesome is unwholesome.

461
00:44:22,531 --> 00:44:26,995
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, by doing these things, you're basically not even trying to look further.

462
00:44:27,656 --> 00:44:33,902
[SPEAKER_03]: By seeing, well, there's more to that principle of wholesome and unwholesome that I'm not seeing.

463
00:44:34,022 --> 00:44:36,984
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you would just be believing that because

464
00:44:38,221 --> 00:44:43,686
[SPEAKER_02]: This is kind of a thing that all monks would agree is good or something.

465
00:44:43,946 --> 00:44:45,607
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it's not bad.

466
00:44:46,248 --> 00:44:51,912
[SPEAKER_02]: So, but that's not really developing the... No, it would be a form of waiting.

467
00:44:52,052 --> 00:44:57,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, as long as I stay within the boundaries of these precepts, sooner or later, that's gonna happen to me.

468
00:44:58,057 --> 00:45:01,941
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, some sort of like a magical waiting for it to come to you.

469
00:45:01,981 --> 00:45:06,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, like things, like effacement takes place just on account of keeping the precepts.

470
00:45:07,125 --> 00:45:07,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

471
00:45:07,637 --> 00:45:13,182
[SPEAKER_01]: That itself would be actually one of the first three fetters that precisely prevents the sotāpanna.

472
00:45:13,222 --> 00:45:13,743
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

473
00:45:14,404 --> 00:45:23,933
[SPEAKER_02]: So, and speaking about that, that's why it's said in the suttas that the sotāpanna is the one who knows wholesome is wholesome and unwholesome is unwholesome.

474
00:45:24,173 --> 00:45:27,156
[SPEAKER_01]: And in other places it's... And the root of the wholesome.

475
00:45:27,196 --> 00:45:27,837
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, the root, yeah.

476
00:45:28,797 --> 00:45:36,043
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, in other places also said that, without grasping the sign of the mind, it's impossible for a person to get the right view.

477
00:45:37,084 --> 00:45:54,717
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, then it says, without delighting in seclusion, if they still delight in company, it's impossible that they will get to see the sign of the mind, because it's not like some mystical revelation of an image or perception that comes to you.

478
00:45:55,377 --> 00:46:06,526
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the recognition of those intentions, like the recognition of those impulses, let's call it that way, that are motivating you to do stuff.

479
00:46:07,367 --> 00:46:15,293
[SPEAKER_03]: A sotāpanna is... or a person who... a person gains sotāpatti by being able to see those intentions and then

480
00:46:16,414 --> 00:46:18,017
[SPEAKER_03]: Those background states.

481
00:46:18,337 --> 00:46:21,642
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, that's where you get to see the Four Noble Truths.

482
00:46:22,483 --> 00:46:26,108
[SPEAKER_03]: When the sutta says, he sees as it is.

483
00:46:27,865 --> 00:46:28,685
[SPEAKER_03]: What is suffering?

484
00:46:29,126 --> 00:46:30,447
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the origin of suffering?

485
00:46:30,908 --> 00:46:32,189
[SPEAKER_03]: What is the cessation of suffering?

486
00:46:32,429 --> 00:46:34,351
[SPEAKER_03]: Where is the path leading to the cessation of suffering?

487
00:46:34,932 --> 00:46:40,757
[SPEAKER_03]: As it is doesn't mean, like, sometimes it would get translated as it truly is, or as it really is.

488
00:46:40,797 --> 00:46:44,942
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you get some sort of hidden revelation.

489
00:46:44,982 --> 00:46:49,927
[SPEAKER_03]: But no, it just means you had these intentions there, and when you were contemplating,

490
00:46:50,607 --> 00:46:54,953
[SPEAKER_03]: What suffering was, or what craving was, it was always kind of over there.

491
00:46:55,113 --> 00:46:58,938
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you weren't seeing this stuff while it was there.

492
00:46:59,058 --> 00:47:01,241
[SPEAKER_03]: This this kind of background mental attitudes.

493
00:47:01,941 --> 00:47:04,324
[SPEAKER_03]: You were not seeing craving on that level.

494
00:47:05,740 --> 00:47:07,341
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the only place where it can be seen.

495
00:47:07,381 --> 00:47:07,982
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

496
00:47:08,042 --> 00:47:14,129
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, you were thinking, oh, the craving is like... Like I said, you started like that.

497
00:47:14,269 --> 00:47:18,194
[SPEAKER_03]: You would have started by thinking, oh, craving is when I go out and do these things.

498
00:47:18,274 --> 00:47:18,614
[SPEAKER_03]: But no.

499
00:47:19,115 --> 00:47:22,018
[SPEAKER_03]: Craving is that background attitude of the mind.

500
00:47:22,058 --> 00:47:22,298
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

501
00:47:23,265 --> 00:47:26,607
[SPEAKER_03]: Recognizing that background attitude of the mind is seeing the sign of the mind.

502
00:47:27,108 --> 00:47:29,309
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why that's indispensable.

503
00:47:29,349 --> 00:47:33,892
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, literally, you absolutely cannot become a sotāpanna without seeing that.

504
00:47:33,932 --> 00:47:37,935
[SPEAKER_03]: Because anything that you think craving is, won't be craving.

505
00:47:38,135 --> 00:47:40,857
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it will be basically, like, secondary.

506
00:47:41,757 --> 00:47:49,823
[SPEAKER_03]: You might be able to see... What most people are seeing, usually, is the... when they think of craving, it's, like, already further

507
00:47:50,492 --> 00:47:52,833
[SPEAKER_03]: It's down the line of where those attitudes come.

508
00:47:52,873 --> 00:48:03,796
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, they might think of all these thoughts and these agitation that I experienced, and then they might get rid of it by doing a relaxation meditation technique.

509
00:48:04,236 --> 00:48:10,318
[SPEAKER_03]: But all of that arose in the first place, because you didn't see the background attitude of your mind.

510
00:48:10,798 --> 00:48:15,880
[SPEAKER_03]: You acted out of it, and that led to all this particular disturbance coming up.

511
00:48:16,700 --> 00:48:26,851
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you had seen that from the very start, then nothing... like, you wouldn't be able to overlook, even if you wanted to, that acting out of this is not for your benefit.

512
00:48:27,292 --> 00:48:33,138
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's like, who on earth would think that, like, if you let a child

513
00:48:34,248 --> 00:48:36,770
[SPEAKER_03]: That's already spoiled and it's throwing a tantrum.

514
00:48:37,270 --> 00:48:42,434
[SPEAKER_03]: Who on earth would think that just letting it keep going that direction is going to result in something good?

515
00:48:43,134 --> 00:48:48,938
[SPEAKER_03]: It just becomes immediately apparent that, no, that there is no benefit that can come from me giving in to this.

516
00:48:49,559 --> 00:48:55,583
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, that's why the Buddha said, if you see one noble truth, you see all of them.

517
00:48:56,223 --> 00:48:59,926
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's all about seeing... all of it just becomes apparent when you see, oh,

518
00:49:01,102 --> 00:49:02,722
[SPEAKER_03]: What am I actually doing here?

519
00:49:03,102 --> 00:49:05,323
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, am I trying to get rid of pleasure?

520
00:49:05,703 --> 00:49:06,503
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I trying...

521
00:49:06,523 --> 00:49:07,903
[SPEAKER_03]: Sorry, am I trying to get more pleasure?

522
00:49:08,023 --> 00:49:09,183
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I trying to get rid of pain?

523
00:49:09,704 --> 00:49:13,404
[SPEAKER_03]: On that background level, anyone can think that they already see that.

524
00:49:13,504 --> 00:49:29,007
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, I know when I'm lustful or angry, but if you're not a sotāpanna, it's because there's still more depth to that recognition of what it even means to try to get rid of pain or go for pleasure.

525
00:49:30,942 --> 00:49:42,831
[SPEAKER_04]: Can we, like, ask, like, because you were saying, like, seeing the sign of the mind is, like, a necessary thing in order to recognize what's wholesome, what's unwholesome.

526
00:49:43,351 --> 00:49:47,374
[SPEAKER_04]: Then you say, like, seclusion is, like, a necessary basis to see the sign of the mind.

527
00:49:47,734 --> 00:49:55,480
[SPEAKER_04]: Can we say, can we clarify, like, why, like, not being into seclusion, like, obstruct us from seeing the sign of the mind?

528
00:49:58,269 --> 00:50:05,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, the the most obvious thing that comes to mind is when you live with a... when you're overly involved with other people.

529
00:50:06,665 --> 00:50:12,186
[SPEAKER_03]: What automatically happens is, whether you recognize it or not, like, that's what, you know, peer pressure and stuff.

530
00:50:12,827 --> 00:50:18,908
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you regard, you would start to be regarding as good, or as, or as, like, what should be done.

531
00:50:20,028 --> 00:50:22,169
[SPEAKER_03]: You would take it on the level of what these other people are doing.

532
00:50:22,689 --> 00:50:29,571
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, it's just a natural kind of human thing, that you measure yourself by what, what people in your environment do.

533
00:50:30,271 --> 00:50:31,911
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's on the most obvious level.

534
00:50:32,031 --> 00:50:34,272
[SPEAKER_03]: But, as well, the, the...

535
00:50:35,591 --> 00:50:46,056
[SPEAKER_03]: The kind of problem with being... with delighting in company is that... that requires you to overlook that background attitude.

536
00:50:46,497 --> 00:50:52,840
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, the fact that you're delighting in company, in and of itself, already means that you're overlooking the background attitude.

537
00:50:52,860 --> 00:50:55,741
[SPEAKER_01]: You are pretending or delighting in something which moves in the other direction.

538
00:50:55,801 --> 00:50:56,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

539
00:50:56,482 --> 00:50:59,123
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're already, like, trying to look away.

540
00:50:59,203 --> 00:51:04,466
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, there's the only way that you can delight in company is because you want to kind of...

541
00:51:05,326 --> 00:51:08,867
[SPEAKER_03]: Get out of this container that is your experience.

542
00:51:08,887 --> 00:51:19,052
[SPEAKER_03]: You want, like, to get out into this other thing out there or other people out there that will, sort of, give you some kind of ease.

543
00:51:20,012 --> 00:51:26,915
[SPEAKER_03]: The same way as you might, you know, drink or you're kind of seeking this external to forget about this situation.

544
00:51:26,975 --> 00:51:30,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Or call someone because you feel lonely or something.

545
00:51:30,156 --> 00:51:32,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, just, you know, by talking,

546
00:51:32,537 --> 00:51:33,457
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the word, yeah.

547
00:51:33,497 --> 00:51:35,138
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the word, yeah.

548
00:51:35,178 --> 00:51:39,720
[SPEAKER_01]: You kind of release the... Yeah.

549
00:51:40,000 --> 00:51:44,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, actually, you can't.

550
00:51:44,421 --> 00:51:48,103
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just as... that is the same as giving in to the child's tantrum.

551
00:51:48,403 --> 00:51:48,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

552
00:51:49,383 --> 00:51:55,467
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one needs to then recognize that, yeah, like, you know, and then... but you see, this is... because mind can play tricks.

553
00:51:55,487 --> 00:51:57,588
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, I'm just calling someone to see how they're doing.

554
00:51:57,648 --> 00:52:01,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, sure, that might be there, but there's something even more fundamental going on there.

555
00:52:02,270 --> 00:52:05,752
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when... so, this has to come with the basic sense of honesty.

556
00:52:05,972 --> 00:52:06,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

557
00:52:06,392 --> 00:52:11,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is the whole authenticity, basically, the discussion feeds into this as well.

558
00:52:11,975 --> 00:52:14,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can't be saved by someone else.

559
00:52:14,917 --> 00:52:15,097
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

560
00:52:16,537 --> 00:52:21,879
[SPEAKER_03]: And even by your own mind, like, you need to have an attitude of almost perpetual suspicion.

561
00:52:22,219 --> 00:52:22,479
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

562
00:52:22,519 --> 00:52:28,401
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you can't, you can't trust that, because your mind is telling you that my motivation is such and such.

563
00:52:28,621 --> 00:52:28,701
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

564
00:52:28,741 --> 00:52:30,602
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your ally, that mind.

565
00:52:30,942 --> 00:52:41,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, but you see, but then that's the thing, because if somebody has established, let's say, that sense of suspicion, that is, right here and now, the birth of sati, of, like,

566
00:52:42,132 --> 00:52:45,793
[SPEAKER_01]: recollection, or mindfulness as it is properly translated.

567
00:52:46,314 --> 00:53:01,980
[SPEAKER_01]: So, rather than signing up to a 10-day group retreat, or a 20-day group retreat, or whatever group retreat, where one assumes I'm going to learn a method and I'm going to gradually become more mindful and aware, that's actually never going to happen.

568
00:53:02,140 --> 00:53:07,542
[SPEAKER_01]: The moment you assume that you can gradually become mindful and aware, you are already overlooking

569
00:53:09,012 --> 00:53:13,596
[SPEAKER_01]: The intention that, right now, you're giving yourself time in order to gradually go there.

570
00:53:13,636 --> 00:53:20,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whereas what you want to see is that you have no choice but to act right here and now.

571
00:53:20,902 --> 00:53:35,293
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you see that you cannot fully trust your mind, but at the same time that you can develop that mind, then it is that attitude of suspiciousness which you just coined it, that is what will give rise to the full-blown mindfulness on the spot.

572
00:53:35,633 --> 00:53:37,595
[SPEAKER_01]: And if somebody has understood just that,

573
00:53:38,095 --> 00:53:44,737
[SPEAKER_01]: For the rest of their life, they never need to go to any group meditation retreat again in order to learn how to be mindful.

574
00:53:44,837 --> 00:53:49,139
[SPEAKER_01]: If that attitude is right, they will see the consequences of this.

575
00:53:49,399 --> 00:53:59,442
[SPEAKER_01]: You will have freed yourself from this whole religious spiritual circus of accumulating this, that and the other, in order to gradually gain what you think is wholesome,

576
00:54:00,202 --> 00:54:01,042
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's never gonna happen.

577
00:54:01,123 --> 00:54:15,630
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but the problem is, see, that comes with a burden now, because when you go to the retreat or wherever it might be, like, it might be difficult in the external sense, like, oh, you have to sit for 15 hours a day or whatever, but nothing.

578
00:54:16,350 --> 00:54:28,399
[SPEAKER_03]: Even the torture, literal physical torture, like the ascetics back in the Buddha's day, they could sleep on beds made of nails and stuff.

579
00:54:28,720 --> 00:54:36,406
[SPEAKER_03]: But nothing is more unpleasant, in that kind of deeply unsettling sense, than questioning

580
00:54:37,006 --> 00:54:37,707
[SPEAKER_03]: your own mind.

581
00:54:37,907 --> 00:54:42,651
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, not going with the grain, when it's that tantrum is there, and not going with it.

582
00:54:43,131 --> 00:54:44,492
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the most unpleasant thing.

583
00:54:44,512 --> 00:54:51,237
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you would free yourself from the external duty of, you know, following these practices and stuff, but

584
00:54:52,618 --> 00:55:00,001
[SPEAKER_03]: At the expense of, right now, like we mentioned before, most people want to be free from suffering ASAP.

585
00:55:00,341 --> 00:55:10,865
[SPEAKER_03]: They're not willing to accept the fact that, you know, the recognition of what's wholesome might initially be unpleasant, because the mind doesn't value that direction.

586
00:55:12,425 --> 00:55:19,868
[SPEAKER_03]: But, see, that would free you, but that's not... it doesn't come for free, and it certainly doesn't go

587
00:55:20,828 --> 00:55:22,369
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same direction that you're used to.

588
00:55:22,449 --> 00:55:24,510
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you're gonna have to kind of break.

589
00:55:25,631 --> 00:55:27,932
[SPEAKER_03]: And when you break, there's always that, you know.

590
00:55:27,992 --> 00:55:28,232
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

591
00:55:28,612 --> 00:55:33,275
[SPEAKER_02]: And it leaves you very much like, you know, on your own in it.

592
00:55:33,715 --> 00:55:38,278
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're fully responsible and that can be overwhelming as well for people.

593
00:55:38,518 --> 00:55:41,980
[SPEAKER_03]: To the extent that you were depending on external safety,

594
00:55:42,861 --> 00:55:52,860
[SPEAKER_03]: This is going to feel unpleasant, because it kind of suddenly, like, sweeps the floor that you were sitting on, sweeps it out.

595
00:55:52,880 --> 00:55:53,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Pulls the rug, yeah.

596
00:55:53,661 --> 00:55:54,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

597
00:55:54,985 --> 00:55:57,087
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's not going to stay unpleasant.

598
00:55:57,347 --> 00:55:58,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

599
00:55:58,148 --> 00:56:10,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, someone needs to understand that it might be, let's say, somewhat of a bitter pill to swallow in the beginning, but when the... when that sort of right effort is put in, then it's not going to stay unpleasant like that.

600
00:56:10,601 --> 00:56:16,807
[SPEAKER_01]: On the contrary, that is where it's going to be... that independence from external circumstances becomes the foundation of the true freedom then.

601
00:56:18,732 --> 00:56:21,173
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how it ties in with the authenticity.

602
00:56:21,434 --> 00:56:39,144
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why authenticity is indispensable for any progress in dhamma, because if you are not willing to always question your mental states, and question that it doesn't matter how right it feels,

603
00:56:40,043 --> 00:56:45,146
[SPEAKER_03]: For you to be doing X, Y or Z, there still might be greed, aversion and delusion in it.

604
00:56:45,846 --> 00:56:57,253
[SPEAKER_03]: If you're not willing to take that on, then... which is what authenticity is, then you will be justifying non-dhamma as dhamma.

605
00:56:57,473 --> 00:57:03,096
[SPEAKER_03]: You will be justifying greed, aversion and delusion as non-greed, non-aversion and non-delusion.

606
00:57:03,637 --> 00:57:07,479
[SPEAKER_03]: Or at least, certainly, you will not know it

607
00:57:08,345 --> 00:57:09,725
[SPEAKER_03]: For sure, for what it is.

608
00:57:09,765 --> 00:57:13,287
[SPEAKER_03]: You might, as it's said in the Sutta, you might, like, I don't do anything bad.

609
00:57:14,247 --> 00:57:18,949
[SPEAKER_03]: But, as the Buddha defined, that's not... because the baby also doesn't do anything bad.

610
00:57:18,989 --> 00:57:19,209
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

611
00:57:19,589 --> 00:57:21,790
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that, that in and of itself, that's not it.

612
00:57:21,850 --> 00:57:22,350
[SPEAKER_03]: That's not it.

613
00:57:22,550 --> 00:57:31,393
[SPEAKER_03]: So, only with that authenticity, you become able to, eh... recognize, again, these, these, eh...

614
00:57:32,446 --> 00:57:36,731
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you can at least have the openness to question yourself.

615
00:57:36,891 --> 00:57:47,222
[SPEAKER_03]: It doesn't mean that you will see things immediately, but you will at least be having, again, that suspiciousness that I mentioned.

616
00:57:47,362 --> 00:57:51,627
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, whatever you think is wholesome, you don't take it for granted, because

617
00:57:54,025 --> 00:57:57,670
[SPEAKER_03]: If you know what is wholesome, you should be free from suffering right now.

618
00:57:57,950 --> 00:57:58,171
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

619
00:57:58,471 --> 00:58:04,719
[SPEAKER_03]: Not like I know what's wholesome and then somehow later on that gives me some result.

620
00:58:05,741 --> 00:58:08,084
[SPEAKER_03]: Knowing what's wholesome frees you from suffering.

621
00:58:09,635 --> 00:58:12,918
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same way, knowing the escape from suffering frees you from suffering.

622
00:58:13,378 --> 00:58:17,101
[SPEAKER_03]: Not like, I know the escape, then I do something, and then I get the escape.

623
00:58:18,121 --> 00:58:22,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding wholesomeness, like right view, really, is where the freedom is.

624
00:58:24,266 --> 00:58:27,228
[SPEAKER_02]: Not right knowledge leads to right liberation.

